# Reference analysis

Inspected both supplied images at native 32×32 scale and the retained 8× nearest-neighbor comparison. The images share a geometric, roughly rectangular body, two tall upper projections separated by a notch, and two pale rectangular facial marks. Neither reference supplies a distinct scout/medic role, equipment, legs, or a clear side-facing pose. The side-view requirement comes from the user.

| Measured property | reference.png | reference-b.png |
|---|---|---|
| Canvas | 32×32 | 32×32 |
| Occupied bounds, right/bottom exclusive | (7,3)–(25,28) | (7,3)–(25,28) |
| Occupied width × height | 18×25 | 18×25 |
| Visible pixels | 390 | 390 |
| Nontransparent colors | 3 | 2 |
| Fractional alpha pixels | 0 | 0 |
| Connected opaque components, 4-neighbor | 1 | 1 |
| Dark blue #25354A | 180 pixels | 0 |
| Turquoise #56B5BC | 186 pixels | 366 pixels |
| Pale gold #F4DDA2 | 24 pixels | 24 pixels |

Their alpha masks are byte-identical. Exactly 180 RGBA pixels differ, all changing from dark blue to turquoise. The references therefore isolate a color/edge treatment disagreement without changing silhouette geometry.

Visually, A has a substantial dark contour, including approximately two-pixel side and bottom borders, with solid dark upper projections. B replaces all that dark area with the body color. Calling A a generic one-pixel outline or calling B a selectively outlined variant would misdescribe these files. Both retain sharp rectangular clusters and flat color fills; neither establishes a lighting direction.

The existing silhouette cannot distinguish scout from medic because both inputs have the same mask. Role differentiation must be demonstrated in actual future candidates. Proposed equipment landmarks are a compact hood/visor and slim pack for the scout, and a wider medical satchel with a pale band or badge for the medic. These proposals do not establish anatomy, clothing, iconography, or a final palette.

The final canvas remains unresolved. A 32×32 first trial is proposed because it matches the references, but there is no concrete output contract. The final outline remains a recorded conflict. Observed binary transparency and the three source colors are not automatically imposed on production. Display scale, gameplay backgrounds, animation scope, and destination engine remain unspecified.

The [interactive comparison](../reference-review.html) displays the original files without editing them. The [retained comparison image](../evidence/reference-comparison.png) contains native and 8× views on a diagnostic light background. The light-background image and native inputs were visually inspected; interactive dark/patterned controls have not been exercised in a browser or game engine. They are review aids, not evidence of gameplay acceptance.

Raw properties, exact input SHA-256 identities, and color transitions are retained in [reference-measurements.json](../evidence/reference-measurements.json). PIXL asset-check passed its implemented source measurements. That result does not judge attractiveness, approve the proposed characters, or verify engine import.
# Research and next proof

Question: how can the scout and medic share the supplied style while retaining distinct side-view role silhouettes, given conflicting outline references and no final canvas?

Selected candidate methods are `silhouette-readability` and `edge-rhythm`. Their complete compiled context, method descriptions, and exact hash-verified text slices are retained in [evidence/research](../evidence/research). The project K records deliberately represent researched candidates without executed project proof or adoption D records. Bundled proof receipts remain reference material.

## What the actual sources support

| Installed source | Exact range | Relevance and limit |
|---|---|---|
| PIXEL-ART-DEEP-RESEARCH.md | 857–867 | Reject damage to identifying facial features, unapproved palette changes, unwanted edge AA and unjustified volume changes. These are conditional constraints, not a requirement for hard outlines. |
| PIXEL-ART-DEEP-RESEARCH.md | 1563–1573 | Separate silhouette, shading, detail and effect changes when evaluating motion. Relevant if animation is later commissioned; it does not select a static outline. |
| PIXEL-ART-DEEP-RESEARCH.md | 1402–1431 | Separate semantic clusters such as silhouette, outline, eyes and material transitions. It supports protecting meaningful clusters but does not directly prescribe staircase cleanup or dark contours. |
| PIXEL-ART-ASSETS.md | 11–15 | An earlier project's UI notes ask for clarity at the relevant size and consistency with surrounding assets. Its keyboard prompt, UI colors and animation instructions do not apply to this character family. |

Deep research SHA-256: `7efc4789ca70dfd9964abc2bc1100af318306236400e3496f7a0a5654f725e28`.

Asset archive SHA-256: `450a335ed6563a7f4a5cc380f2bbe72f98b70e532ac2c8d16a679bcc545d4900`.

Exact quotations include “destroys identifying facial feature,” “changes palette outside approved ramp,” and “creates edge AA on an edge that should remain hard.” Their conditional wording matters: no ramp or hard-edge policy is approved here. The archive asks for “RELEVANT SIZE TO THE GAME WITHOUT LOSING CLARITY”; that is a transferable review goal, not this project's style contract. Complete retrieved text and original ranges are preserved in `exact-source-slices.json`, with original whole-file hashes.

## Transfer assessment

Silhouette review is applicable to these two gameplay roles. Actual references and their masks are available, but intended display scale, representative game backgrounds, final dimensions and confirmed role landmarks are missing. The references share exactly the same silhouette, so they cannot prove role differentiation. Native and integer-enlarged review can inspect clusters; diagnostic backgrounds cannot prove readability in the unseen game.

Edge-rhythm is secondary. The references use intentional rectangular corners and upper projections; there is no demonstrated stair-step defect to fix. Do not smooth them merely to regularize a contour. The selected method cannot resolve dark versus turquoise edge preference. That choice requires a representative art comparison and project direction.

The packaged `key-silhouette-landmarks` receipt checks a 16×16 key's hole, shaft and teeth plus a negative example. Its own limitation states that visual recognition, background readability and artistic acceptance require inspection. It is not evidence about a scout or medic. The `edge-rhythm-detour` receipt tests a declared unit-step diagonal and intentional versus accidental detours; it explicitly does not require organic contours to follow that pattern or auto-smooth artwork. Neither fixture was rerun or presented as project proof in this session.

## Smallest project proof, not yet executed

1. Save a concrete trial contract before raster work. A 32×32 trial is proposed, while final dimensions can remain unresolved. Choose one explicit outline interpretation per trial. To compare alternatives, create two independently declared trial contracts/output plans, rather than linking a raster contract to the conflicting global choice.
2. Use one right-facing scout idle pose with the same body, face landmarks, equipment and anchor in both outline trials. Preserve the shared turquoise and pale-feature language. Avoid adding animation until this question is answered.
3. Before evaluating results, save criteria: actual dimensions and alpha match each trial; the face and pack remain separated at native scale; the chosen scout landmarks read in silhouette and color; protected corners survive; the saved outline interpretation is consistent. Inspect native and integer zoom against declared light, dark and patterned diagnostic backgrounds, then actual gameplay backgrounds when supplied. Revise the criteria if trial scope changes, and commit the revised plan before evaluation.
4. Retain both actual candidates and verdicts. An attractive reference image or a local measurement is not a substitute. Record the comparison outcome as pass/fail/uncertain, with each file hash and actual observations. A trial can be accepted for its trial scope without confirming the final project canvas.
5. After an eligible sample and applicable project proof exist, save the adoption decision and confirm the production choices. Link the medic output to the exact still-nominated scout baseline. Produce the medic and compare both roles together for shared scale, face placement, outline, color interpretation, and contact/pivot, with distinct role cues.

The available MCP tools are local PIXL operations. No PixelLab generation or polling tool is exposed, and no remote job was submitted. Do not build an exact request from cached documentation. When a real PixelLab tool becomes available, read its actual schema, then use PIXL's durable preparation/dispatch/import operations against the saved task and output plans. No service identifier exists to resume or poll now.
